# ============================== YEAR 10 ==============================
R(10, "Some fields sulk", "heaven")
act("Paddy turns the ninth I Ching card: Heaven Has Favourites. A good harvest in Province 5; a bad one in Provinces 1, 6 and 7; no disasters. The population holds at 300 million, x3. This year's incomes: Graham -30, Reina +60, Paddy +30, Silke +50. The crown holds 1.5 and no realia, so the harvest would leave it at -28.5, in debt. Before the harvest is paid out, the table may give or lend grain.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Paddy", "\"Heaven Has Favourites. Some fields shine, some fields sulk.\" Provinces two, four and five have a good harvest. One, three, six, seven and eight have a bad one. Population stays at three hundred million, times three. Reina, the sky's on your side of the coast this year, and the rest of us get the rot again.",
 "Paddy: That's a fourth bad year on my paddies, and Reina gets a good one just when all she needs is Silke's Scroll. Whatever Silke does with that Scroll now decides the whole coast, and I mean to watch it closely.")
act("The Storyteller notes: an appointed reader's salary is the Emperor's choice; the 'at least 5' is a seated Scholar's due.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Graham", "Here's the year in numbers. The crown has one and a half and owes thirty, so it's twenty-eight and a half short. Reina, your coast just made sixty: lend the crown twenty-nine now and you get thirty-one back from this year's tax, not next year's. Here's where the money goes, so nobody's surprised. You earn a hundred and forty between you; at thirty percent that's forty-two, and the crown owes thirty-one to the lender, fifteen to Silke and nine in salaries. Forty percent covers all of it with one grain to spare, and that's the rate I'll set. I'm telling you now, before anybody's paid, so it isn't a surprise later.",
 "Graham: The crown can't earn its way out, because the provinces earn it and I can only tax them. So I borrow against this year's tax, name the rate before the harvest, and the table can see every grain is going back to them.")
say("Reina", "Twenty-nine, Graham, and thirty-one back out of this year's tax, at the forty percent you've just told everyone. You paid Silke when you said you would and you paid me what you promised as reader, so I'll take your word on this one. Nobody at this table gains from a crown in debt, least of all whoever sits there next.",
 "Reina: If the crown ends up with me, I'd rather take over a solvent crown than one that's twenty-eight and a half in debt. And even if it doesn't, I end this year two grain up. The only thing between me and the throne now is the Scroll on Silke's shelf and twenty grain, and the coast just made sixty.")
act("The Storyteller notes: the crown hasn't yet repaid Silke's 15; it is due from this year's tax.", map_hl=[1,7])
act("Reina lends the crown 29 before the harvest, on Graham's terms: 31 back from this year's tax.", "Grain may be lent at any time, and no deal is binding (Section 5).", {pid(5): -29, pid(1): 29}, map_hl=[1,5], deal=True)
harvest("Silke pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile; a negative income is paid into it. With Reina's loan the crown covers its -30 and ends at 0.5, out of debt.")
say("Graham", "Forty percent, the rate I named before the harvest: fifty-six grain. Here's where it goes. Thirty-one back to Reina, fifteen back to Silke, nine in salaries, and the crown ends the year on one and a half, owing nobody. The crown will pay half a grain a square on new land, for up to three squares, and one grain toward a canal section if Reina or Paddy wants to dig one.",
 "Graham: Every grain I took in goes back out to the table that lent it, and the crown ends the year in credit. I'd rather owe nobody than sit on a full chest, because a crown that pays its debts gives nobody a reason to revolt.")
taxes(.40, collector="Paddy collects the tax for the crown")
act("Graham repays both loans from the tax: 31 to Reina (29 lent this year, and 2 more) and 15 to Silke (13 lent last year, and 2 more).", "No deal is binding, but this one is kept (Section 5).", {pid(1): -46, pid(5): 31, pid(7): 15}, map_hl=[1,5,7], deal=True)
act("Graham's offers for the works step: one section of the Grand Canal in Province 5 or 6 (40), the province paying 39 and the crown 1, or nothing built this year; and 0.5 a square from the crown on new land bought by any province this year, for three squares at most in all.", map_hl=[1,5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Thank you, Graham, the crown's books balance and it paid every grain it owed this year, which is more than I managed in my reign. No canal from me and no sea. The coast is fed and the coast is solvent, and I'm keeping my grain where it is.",
 "Reina: The Scroll has to go up this year and Silke sets its price, so every grain I keep raises the most she'd have to ask to shut me out. Ninety, less the twenty to claim, means she'd have to set her minimum above seventy, and three quarters of that would go into the Pile.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you for the offer, Graham, but thirty-nine of mine for a ditch with the crown's name on it is more than my paddies can carry. Reina said it well last year. I'll take one more square of sea at R27, though, with your half grain, and I'm grateful for it.",
 "Paddy: I couldn't cover the canal anyway, and every work that gets built brings the end of the game closer, which doesn't suit a man who's this far behind. One more field means another twenty a year if that times-six card ever turns up.")
say("Silke", "The canal's for the coast to decide, not me. I'll take two of your half-grain squares, Graham, and that leaves the third for whoever wants it. If times six comes, Province 7 will be eating.",
 "Silke: Two more fields cover most of the times-six risk and leave me plenty of grain. The real fight this year is my Land Deed Scroll, the last piece Reina needs, and I set its price.")
act("Public works: nothing is built this year; neither Reina nor Paddy would pay 39 of a canal section. The Great Wall (160) and the Grand Canal (160) are still owed.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
buy_land(6, ["R27"], {6:19.5, 1:0.5}, "New land: Province 6 buys one square of sea, R27, for 20: Paddy pays 19.5 and the crown 0.5. It yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
buy_land(7, ["U15", "V14"], {7:39, 1:1}, "New land: Province 7 buys two squares of the Warlord's land, U15 and V14, for 40: Silke pays 39 and the crown 1, 0.5 a square. Each yields 20 from next year's harvest.")
act("The auctions: only one piece is left in stock, Province 7's Land Deed Scroll, so it is the year's only lot; Silke sets its minimum. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5,6,7])
say("Reina", "Silke, it's your Scroll and your price, and everyone here can do the sums: I have ninety, and the claim takes twenty. Set it wherever you think is fair. You gave me the Vase for two, and I'm not going to haggle with you over the last piece.",
 "Reina: Seventy is everything I can spend and still keep the twenty I need to claim. Anything above that and Silke has shut the door on me, and three quarters of whatever I pay goes into the Pile whichever way it falls.")
say("Paddy", "I've a Scroll of my own already sitting on R23, so I'll not bid on this one. I won't run anyone up for a piece I've no use for. Reina, whatever you do with it, I'd ask you to remember the Rice Peasant has kept his word to everyone at this table.",
 "Paddy: Reina's got ninety grain and only needs this Scroll, so me bidding eight and a half would just push more grain into the Pile and wouldn't stop her. I'd sooner have her good will when she climbs than have her remember I put up a fight over a piece I don't need.")
say("Silke", "The last Scroll in China, and everyone knows whose shelf it finishes. Seventy-five, Reina. You told me once to name a price where it hurts, and I listened.",
 "Silke: Reina has ninety grain and her set needs twenty grain on top of the Scroll. At seventy-five she either doesn't buy it, or she buys it and can't climb this year. It costs me one year of holding it, and that might be the whole game.")
act("The Land Deed Scroll goes unsold: Silke's minimum was 75 and Reina's limit 70; Paddy passed. It stays in Province 7's stock and must go up again next year at 2.", "An unsold lot reopens at 2 next round (R: An unsold lot reopens at 2).", map_hl=[5,7])
act("The Storyteller notes: there are two Land Deed Scrolls, the one in Province 7's stock and Paddy's on R23.", map_hl=[6,7])
salaries({6:6, 7:3}, text="Graham pays Paddy 6 for the tax and the I Ching, and Silke 3 for the bank, as he said.", hover="Appointees are paid only if the Emperor chooses. The crown ends the year at 0, owing nobody.")
